From 32d9541fd947fd816b7523ad0bca99122ab236cc Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: =?UTF-8?q?Ville=20Kivel=C3=A4?= <vkivela@hotmail.com>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 18:22:20 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] extensions: let a profile go while a message waits for a
 worker

A message to an extension's service worker waits in the worker's task
queue until the worker is started for it, and every message does: the
start is a round trip even when the worker is already running. Deleting
the profile in that wait crashed the application on a CHECK.

~ProfileQt destroys the profile's keyed services, and marks the context
destroyed between shutting them down and deleting them. The task queue
drops its waiting messages as it is deleted, so each message's port is
destroyed after the mark. ~ExtensionMessagePort asks the browser client
whether the context is still valid before reporting to MessageTracker,
and ExtensionsBrowserClientQt::IsValidContext answered true for any
pointer, so the port looked MessageTracker up on a destroyed context:

  DependencyManager::AssertContextWasntDestroyed
  KeyedServiceTemplatedFactory::GetServiceForContext
  ExtensionMessagePort::~ExtensionMessagePort
  MessageService::OpenChannelParams::~OpenChannelParams
  (the task queue's pending tasks, erased)

Chrome answers from ProfileManager::IsValidProfile, which a profile
leaves before it is destroyed. ProfileQt now keeps the set of profiles
whose destruction has not begun, and leaves it on the first line of its
destructor; IsValidContext answers from that set. Callers pass pointers
that may already be freed, so it compares addresses and follows none.
Every caller of IsValidContext in the extensions layer uses it to skip
work for a context that is going, as it does in Chrome.

The notification case, theReadersAnswerToANotificationReachesTheExtension,
crashed this way in about one run in fifteen: its last poll sends the
worker a message that cleanup() can catch still waiting. The new case
holds a message there every time, with a worker that is slow to start.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
